Vance Oduya eats his supper standing at the dock office counter, a heel of flatbread and a smear of the same pepper paste everyone on the ring is rationing. He wipes his thumb on his trouser leg before touching the berth ledger, then touches it anyway, because the ink has dried. The cancelled moorings sit in their neat column, three of them, each paid up and unused. He squares the ledger against the edge of the desk, twice, until the corner lines up. Then he reaches over and switches off the lamp.

